<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1527–1543</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ahmad ibn Ibrahim, Imam Ahmad, Ahmad Gragn, Ahmad Gran</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a>, known as Ahmad Gragn, was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> and military leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> who led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> from 1527 to 1543. Using firearms and Ottoman support, he conquered much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian highlands</a>. He ruled through nominal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma</a> sultans, and his death in battle in 1543 against an Ethiopian and Portuguese force ended Adal's ascendancy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi.jpg" alt="Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ugaas Raage · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imaam_Ahmed_Gurey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
